\trowd\trgaph108\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx3120\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx6240\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx9360{\pard\intbl\sa0 {\f1 rtf.py}\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0 \cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0 Writes the .rtf twin (Times New Roman 12) of every document the machine produces; {\f1 mark1 rtf} does the same for the hand-written ones. The .md is the source.\cell}\row
\trowd\trgaph108\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx3120\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx6240\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx9360{\pard\intbl\sa0 {\f1 inject.py}\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0 I.4\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0 Defected copies of a record and the evaluation that checks each defect is found, against the findings the unaltered record already has.\cell}\row
\trowd\trgaph108\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx3120\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx6240\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\cellx9360{\pard\intbl\sa0 {\f1 texmd.py}\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0 A1\cell}{\pard\intbl\sa0 The author's helper, {\f1 mark1 import-tex}: a LaTeX paper into {\f1 paper/} and {\f1 appendix/} in the protocol's Markdown. Sections, theorem environments, labelled paragraphs and proofs become headings with ids; mathematics stays as written; citations come from the .bib; drawings are dropped with their captions kept; {\f1 --choices} and {\f1 --does-not-claim} name the passages carried into the scope-and-choices section. Not a published object; the author reads and edits what it writes.\cell}\row{\pard\sa160\par}
{\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b The model-judged checks, answered from a chat session\par}
{\pard\sa160 A model-judged check does not call a model. It writes a prompt bundle per repetition:\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 <run>/bundles/<check>/request.json      nonce, k, hashes of the instructions and the data\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 <run>/bundles/<check>/k1/prompt.md      published instructions + response contract + the record, fenced\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 <run>/bundles/<check>/k1/response.json  written by whoever answers\par}
{\pard\f1\fs20\sa0 <run>/bundles/<check>/k1/transcript.md  their reasoning\par}
{\pard\sa160\par}
{\pard\sa160 The instructions come verbatim from {\f1 1-journal/checks/prompts/<check>.md}. The record goes inside a fence marked with a random nonce, labelled as data. The run stops with "N bundles waiting"; once they are answered, {\f1 mark1 check --resume <run-id>} validates every answer against {\f1 1-journal/protocol/schemas/}, checks the nonce, drops any finding whose pointer does not resolve, computes agreement across the k answers against {\f1 1-journal/tolerance.yaml}, and finishes the report. {\f1 machine/llm/HOW-TO-ANSWER.md} is the brief for the answering session. A changed record invalidates the bundles: the hashes no longer match.\par}
{\pard\sa160 {\f1 mark1 replay <run-id>} re-runs a finished run from its stored answers and reports any difference. Zero differences is the repeatability demonstration for everything but the model's own sampling.\par}
{\pard\sa160 Nothing in Mark 1 calls a model service, and nothing here offers one as a way to run it. If a journal asks for it in Stage 2, a connection would sit behind the same interface ({\f1 llm/__init__.py: Backend.ask}) and write the same three files, so replay and the tests would not change.\par}
{\pard\sb240\sa120\keepn\b Limits stated plainly\par}
{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 \'95\tab {\f1 sandbox.py} runs each anchor in a fresh interpreter with an empty working directory, a scrubbed environment and a ten-second timeout. That isolates crashes and hangs. It is not a jail: on Windows it does not stop network or filesystem access by the code under test.\par}
{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 \'95\tab Author-supplied expressions are parsed only after a token whitelist ({\f1 sym.py}): declared symbols, a few functions, numbers and arithmetic. No attribute access, strings, brackets or builtins reach {\f1 eval}.\par}
{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 \'95\tab A signature in this repository is a name, a time and the hashes of the files signed. It records what was signed; it is not cryptographic.\par}
{\pard\li360\fi-360\sa60 \'95\tab The bundle backend does not see token counts, so cost is reported as the number of answers.\par}
